# {{ .prefix }}-subagent-driven-development

<!-- awf:section positioning -->
The subagent implementation chain iterates phases. It preserves sequential implementation dispatch and exclusive implementation-tool batching.
<!-- awf:end -->
<!-- awf:section per-task-review-note -->
**Phase review is report-only.** The parent settles findings after the phase-closing commit.
<!-- awf:end -->
<!-- awf:section when-to-invoke -->
## When to invoke

Invoke when a plan phase benefits from delegated implementation ownership. A plan may mix modes; hand inline phases to `{{ .prefix }}-executing-plans`.
<!-- awf:end -->

## Procedure
<!-- awf:section procedure-resolve-plan -->
1. This skill operates only inside an existing confirmed effort and never creates a missing effort. If ownership is absent, stop and return to mandatory first-creation three-field confirmation. Existing efforts resume under their fixed identity without title reconfirmation only while work remains within their confirmed outcome. Resolve the mutable plan and read every declared phase mode. Before dispatch, validate the fixed identity, one immutable effort slug and exact `.awf/efforts/<slug>/memory.md` path, confirm either legacy `Effort: <slug>` or canonical `effort: <slug>` identity, and preserve one user-managed writer. Repository authority outranks checkpoint prose, and standalone memory is forbidden.
<!-- awf:end -->
<!-- awf:section procedure-raise-concerns -->
2. You, the dispatching parent, stop before dispatch when the plan is missing phase context, paths, boundaries, checks, or a closing subject. Resolve it with the user; never hand a child an incomplete phase and let it discover the gap.
<!-- awf:end -->
<!-- awf:section procedure-extract-context -->
3. Per `{{ .layout.maintainableCodeDesign }}`, preserve the plan's settled structural choices, including a bounded enabling refactor; reassess them if grounded source contradicts them and stop and escalate rather than accept a bolt-on workaround. Extract the complete phase: goal, ordered tasks, exact paths, semantic boundary and ownership, external/internal representations and their translation point, allowed dependency direction, preparatory-refactor decision, prohibited bolt-on shortcuts, validation expectations, dependencies, prior phase commits, checks, closing subject, and the phase's batch of the ADR's declared State changes operations (V2). The owner runs `awf context <the phase's exact paths>` before editing.
<!-- awf:include context-spill -->
The owner preserves the settled design or reports invalidating source facts; does not replan, broaden the task, or perform unrelated cleanup.
<!-- awf:end -->
<!-- awf:section dispatch-conventions -->
4. For a `subagent-driven` phase, verify a known clean and green baseline. A fresh phase owner may run `awf read plan <plan> <P>` for the executable closure. A task projection's generated scope notice, Phase close, and Advances/Completes outcomes are phase-owner context only; they never transfer commit, review, checkpoint, handoff, helper, or outcome authority; projection changes neither ownership nor checkpoint boundaries. Call one implementation child alone in its parent tool batch{{ if .targetSubagentTools }} with `allowCommits: true`{{ else }} and state commit-capable phase-owner mode in the brief{{ end }}, and provide the complete phase plus effort slug and exact owned memory path. The child is the `implementer` agent, whose rendered contract already governs its authority mode, scope discipline, green obligation, escalation, owner transaction, and return shape, so the brief carries the phase rather than restating that contract. The child never edits parent memory. {{ if .targetSubagentTools }}Choose the smallest reliable tier - `small` (narrow, mechanical), `standard` (substantive but bounded), or `large` (broad, intricate, cross-cutting, or high-consequence) - escalating after uncertainty, failed reasoning, or widened scope; omit the `model` field to use configured role routing, overriding deliberately with the tier's exact `provider/model-id`. Never pass `default`, `auto`, or `inherit parent` as a model value. Full tier definitions: the agent guide's workflow section.{{ else }}Choose the smallest reliable tier - `small` (narrow, mechanical), `standard` (substantive but bounded), or `large` (broad, intricate, cross-cutting, or high-consequence) - escalating after uncertainty, failed reasoning, or widened scope; select the smallest reliable target-native model explicitly, or use the harness default and note in the dispatch brief that explicit selection is unavailable. Full tier definitions: the agent guide's workflow section.{{ end }} The commit-capable owner performs all ordered work, stages the complete transaction, and creates the declared phase-closing commit, which requires `awf check staged` and {{ with .vars.gateCmd }}`{{ . }}`{{ else }}the project's gate{{ end }} to pass; a wired pre-commit hook enforces both at commit time, and the owner runs them manually first only in a clone without wired hooks (checkable with `git config core.hooksPath`; when in doubt, run both manually). Never dispatch concurrent implementers or helpers as phase owners.
<!-- awf:end -->
<!-- awf:section procedure-status-handling -->
5. Validate the reported commit and clean checkout, then dispatch one report-only phase review. {{ if .targetSubagentTools }}Choose the smallest reliable tier - `small` (narrow, mechanical), `standard` (substantive but bounded), or `large` (broad, intricate, cross-cutting, or high-consequence) - escalating after uncertainty, failed reasoning, or widened scope; omit the `model` field to use configured role routing, overriding deliberately with the tier's exact `provider/model-id`. Never pass `default`, `auto`, or `inherit parent` as a model value. Full tier definitions: the agent guide's workflow section.{{ else }}Choose the smallest reliable tier - `small` (narrow, mechanical), `standard` (substantive but bounded), or `large` (broad, intricate, cross-cutting, or high-consequence) - escalating after uncertainty, failed reasoning, or widened scope; select the smallest reliable target-native model explicitly, or use the harness default and note in the dispatch brief that explicit selection is unavailable. Full tier definitions: the agent guide's workflow section.{{ end }} The parent owns focused settlement commits, remains the memory writer, and checkpoints only after findings resolve; the report-only reviewer never edits memory.

<!-- awf:include checkpoint-routine -->

If an owner stops dirty, inventory `git status --short`, diff, completed and remaining work, and failed checks. Choose parent completion, restore the known green baseline and redispatch the complete revised phase, or stop for user input. An explicit transfer carries the complete revised phase, dirty-state inventory, completed and remaining work, and recovery verification; a blind successor instruction for one task is forbidden.
<!-- awf:end -->
<!-- awf:section per-task-review -->
6. Review is report-only and phase-level; review fixes remain parent-owned and do not recreate the original transaction.
<!-- awf:end -->
<!-- awf:section final-task-adr-flip -->
7. Apply every explicit V2 batch, including the final batch, atomically with matching claims and lifecycle events in the owning phase. Terminal review owns only the status-only Implemented flip after the applicable review settles.
<!-- awf:end -->
<!-- awf:section terminal-step -->
8. After all settled phases, invoke `{{ .prefix }}-reviewing-impl`.
<!-- awf:end -->

## Notes
<!-- awf:section notes -->
- Sequential dispatch only, never parallel. Helpers remain sequential, commit-disabled, path-disjoint, and parent-integrated.
- Expected output is non-negotiable.
<!-- awf:end -->
<!-- awf:section red-flags -->
## Red flags

Do not casually dispatch over a dirty checkout, infer plan-wide mode, or let a helper own a shared file or closing commit.
<!-- awf:end -->
